3.2.5 Other Architectural Decisions
Beyond the high-level choices, several other deliberate decisions shape the platform's behavior and structure, including advanced ML/AI integration, comprehensive security frameworks, and sophisticated service orchestration patterns.

Architectural Decision
Rationale & Justification
Key Benefits

Use of a Relational Database (PostgreSQL)
While a time-series database could also be suitable, a relational model was chosen for its powerful and flexible querying capabilities. The structured nature of DIS PDUs fits well into the fixed schema of tables like entity_state_record, fire_event_record, and 10+ additional PDU record types. This allows the Data Acquisition Service to perform complex aggregations, joins, and time-series analysis required for generating detailed metrics and supporting ML feature engineering for the CAP PDU Prediction Service. PostgreSQL's ACID compliance ensures data integrity for both transactional operations and analytical workloads.
Powerful Querying (SQL)
Strong Data Consistency
Mature and Well-Supported Ecosystem
ML/AI Feature Engineering Support

Implementation of Explicit Fallback Mechanisms
Services are designed to be resilient to downstream failures with comprehensive error handling patterns. The RealTimeMetricsService includes try-catch blocks that handle exceptions when communicating with the Data Ingestion Service, returning default fallback metrics objects instead of failing requests. The CAP PDU Prediction Service implements graceful degradation when external services are unavailable, using cached data or generating fallback responses. This prevents failures in one microservice from causing cascading failures across the platform.
Increased System Resilience
Graceful Degradation of Service
Prevents Cascading Failures
Enhanced User Experience

Use of Data Transfer Objects (DTOs) for APIs
All services implement comprehensive DTO patterns for API responses, decoupling public API contracts from internal database schemas. The Data Acquisition Service uses specialized DTOs (AggregatedMetricsOverview, MonthlyAggregation, CustomRangeAggregation, TimeBucketAggregation) for different response types. The CAP PDU Prediction Service uses ML-specific DTOs (PredictionRequest, PredictionResponse, ChatRequest, ChatResponse) for AI interactions. This approach allows schema evolution without breaking client integrations and enhances security by exposing only necessary data.
Decoupled API from Database Schema
Improved Security & Control
Tailored and Stable API Contracts
API Versioning Support

Decoupled File-Based Logging
Each microservice is configured to write logs to local files within containers, providing high-performance logging without blocking on network I/O. This pattern integrates with Kubernetes log forwarding agents (like Fluentd) for centralized log collection and analysis. The approach supports comprehensive debugging across microservices while maintaining application performance and enabling distributed tracing capabilities for complex request flows spanning multiple services.
High-Performance Application Logging
Decoupling from Logging Infrastructure
Enables Centralized Log Management
Distributed Tracing Support

Kong Gateway as Unified API Management
The platform migrated from traditional Kubernetes Ingress to Kong Gateway to provide enterprise-grade API management capabilities. Kong Gateway serves as the unified entry point for all HTTP(S) traffic across local, staging, and production environments. It implements sophisticated routing, Firebase JWT authentication through custom jwt-firebase plugin, CORS policies, rate limiting (environment-specific: staging 5000/min, production 10000/min), and comprehensive monitoring. This centralized approach ensures consistent security and API behavior across all services while offloading cross-cutting concerns from individual microservices.
Centralized Security & Authentication
Consistent API Behavior
Enterprise-Grade Features
Simplified Service Development

Factory Pattern for Extensible PDU Processing
The Data Processing Service implements a comprehensive factory pattern (PduParserFactory) to handle 10+ different DIS PDU types. Each PDU type has a specialized parser (EntityStatePduParser, FirePduParser, CollisionPduParser, etc.) implementing the PduParser interface. This design enables easy addition of new PDU types without modifying existing code, supports type-specific optimization, and maintains clean separation of concerns. The DefaultPduParser handles unknown PDU types gracefully, ensuring system stability.
Highly Extensible Design
Type-Specific Optimization
Clean Code Architecture
Future-Proof Implementation

Single-Instance ML Service Deployment
The CAP PDU Prediction Service is configured with a fixed single replica (maxReplicas: 1) to maintain ML model state consistency and avoid conflicts between multiple model instances. This decision ensures coherent vector embeddings, consistent RAG system behavior, and reliable model performance. The service uses higher resource allocation (CPU 300m-1000m, Memory 1.5Gi-2Gi, Ephemeral Storage 2Gi-3Gi) and extended health check timeouts (180s initial delay) to accommodate ML model loading and initialization requirements.
ML Model State Consistency
Reliable AI Performance
Optimized Resource Allocation
Simplified Model Management

Multi-Language Technology Stack
The platform strategically employs multiple programming languages optimized for specific use cases: Java/Spring Boot for enterprise microservices (data-ingestion, data-processing, data-acquisition, cap-user-service) providing robust web APIs, database integration, and Kafka messaging; Python/Flask for the CAP PDU Prediction Service to leverage the rich ML/AI ecosystem including scikit-learn, transformers, sentence-transformers, FAISS, and PyTorch. This approach maximizes technology strengths while maintaining service cohesion through standardized API contracts and Kong Gateway integration.
Technology Optimization
Rich Ecosystem Access
Specialized Tool Usage
Maintained Service Cohesion

Comprehensive Testing Strategy
The platform implements a multi-layered testing approach with 75+ unit and integration tests across all services. Each service includes controller tests (@WebMvcTest), service tests with mock repositories, and integration tests using Testcontainers for database testing. The CAP PDU Prediction Service includes ML model validation and RAG system testing. Quality assurance integrates JaCoCo for code coverage reporting, SonarCloud for static code analysis, and automated DAST security scanning in staging environments. This comprehensive approach ensures code quality, security, and reliability across the entire platform.
High Code Quality
Security Assurance
Reliable Deployments
Comprehensive Coverage

Environment-Specific Configuration Management
The platform employs Kustomize overlays to manage environment-specific configurations across local development, staging, and production environments. Base configurations provide common settings, while overlays apply environment-specific patches: local development uses NodePort services and simplified authentication; staging includes moderate security and DAST scanning; production features enhanced security, higher rate limits, and high-availability configurations. This approach enables consistent base functionality while allowing environment-specific optimizations without configuration drift.
Configuration Consistency
Environment-Specific Optimization
Deployment Reliability
Reduced Configuration Drift

Advanced Resource Management Strategy
Each service has fine-tuned resource requests and limits based on workload characteristics: lightweight services (Data Ingestion: 50m-200m CPU, 256Mi-512Mi memory), standard services (Data Processing, Acquisition, User Service: 100m-300m CPU, 384Mi-768Mi memory), ML service (Prediction Service: 300m-1000m CPU, 1.5Gi-2Gi memory, 2Gi-3Gi ephemeral storage), and gateway service (Kong Gateway: 200m-500m CPU, 512Mi-1Gi memory). HPA configurations use service-specific thresholds (70-85% CPU utilization) to optimize scaling behavior for different workload patterns.
Efficient Resource Utilization
Predictable Performance
Quality of Service Guarantees
Workload-Specific Optimization

